<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mstislav Mstislavich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1228">1228</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Mstislav the Bold, Mstislav Udatny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mstislav Mstislavich, called the Bold (Udatny), was a Rus' prince who ruled Novgorod at intervals from 1210 to 1217 and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-of-rostov">Konstantin of Rostov</a>, he defeated Yuri and Yaroslav of Vladimir at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lipitsa">Battle of Lipitsa</a> in 1216. He was among the princes defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river">Battle of the Kalka River</a> in 1223, and died in 1228.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mstislav Mstislavich was the son of Mstislav Rostislavich, called the Brave, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, and a princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>; his grandfather was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostislav-i-of-kiev">Rostislav I of Kiev</a>. In 1193 and 1203 he was praised for his bravery in the Kipchak wars, which made his name across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, and at about that time he married Maria, a daughter of the Kipchak khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koten">Kotyan</a>. In 1209 he is recorded as ruler of Toropets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A year later he took the throne of Novgorod and seized the men of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-vsevolodovich">Sviatoslav Vsevolodovich</a>. On the way he relieved Torzhok from a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod III</a> of Vladimir. He led two successful Novgorodian campaigns against the Chudes in 1212 and 1214, and in 1215 he drove Vsevolod IV out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and set his uncle Mstislav Romanovich on its throne. After the victory at Lipitsa in 1216 he made his ally Konstantin of Rostov Grand Prince of Vladimir and married his daughter to Yaroslav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>. Meanwhile his enemies deposed him in Novgorod, and he left northern Rus' for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>.</p><p>In 1219 he made peace with his chief rival, Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, who married his daughter Anna. At the Kalka River in 1223, where a coalition of perhaps 18 princes and their Cuman allies fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, he is the only prince named among the nine or so who escaped; he cut loose the boats on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> so that he could not be followed. In 1224 the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-white">Leszek the White</a> defeated him in the Polish-Ruthenian War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mstislav ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> until 1227, when intrigues of the boyars forced him to leave the city to his son-in-law, Andrew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He withdrew to Torchesk, where he died. Through his daughters he was the maternal grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, Prince of Novgorod and Grand Prince of Vladimir, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-i-of-galicia">Leo I of Galicia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mstislav Mstislavich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Internal wars proved as costly as foreign ones: at the Battle of Lipitsa in 1216 <b>Mstislav the Bold of Novgorod</b> and Konstantin of Rostov routed the brothers Yuri and Yaroslav, with heavy losses on the Suzdalian side.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Torchesk, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.68,30.64&amp;year=1218" aria-label="Torchesk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5254 433 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mstislav Mstislavich"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5704 703h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5704 703h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.68,30.64&amp;year=1218">What was here in 1218? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
